<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gelderland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guelderland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gelderland is a province in the east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnhem">Arnhem</a>. It descends from the medieval county and later Duchy of Guelders, which Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> acquired in 1543. As one of the seven provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> it had a vote in the States General, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluwe">Veluwe</a> heathland and fertile river lands made it largely agricultural. In 1672 French armies overran it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gelderland.jpg" alt="Gelderland" width="320" height="361" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gelderland_in_the_Netherlands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The province takes its name from the German city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a> and descends from the County of Guelders, which arose in the 11th century out of the Frankish pagus Hamaland around castles near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roermond">Roermond</a> and Geldern. According to the Wichard saga, the Lords of Pont killed a dragon in 878 and named the town they founded after its death-rattle, Gelre.</p><p>The counts acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betuwe">Betuwe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluwe">Veluwe</a> regions and, through marriage, the County of Zutphen. Through their control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijssel">IJssel</a> rivers they became an important power of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, committed to the interests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and to expansion to the south and west.</p><p>After the acquisition of the imperial city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> in the 13th century, Emperor Louis IV raised the county to a duchy in 1339. From 1379 the duchy was ruled from Jülich and by the counts of Egmond and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>. It resisted Burgundian domination, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">William, Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a> was forced to cede it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1543, and it became part of the Burgundian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands.</p><p>The duchy rose with the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> in 1579. After Philip's deposition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> lay with the States of Gelderland, and the princes of Orange were stadtholders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> temporarily occupied the province in 1672, and in 1713 the southeastern part, including the ducal capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a>, fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><p>Gelderland was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> from 1795 to 1806, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a>'s Kingdom of Holland from 1806 to 1810 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> from 1810, and became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a> in 1815. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnhem">Arnhem</a> was fought there between Allied paratroopers, British XXX Corps and the German II SS Panzer Corps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gelderland is in the centre-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and is the largest province by land area, covering 5,136 km2 of which 176 km2 is water. Its capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnhem">Arnhem</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> and Apeldoorn are larger. It has four regions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluwe">Veluwe</a>, the largest forest region in the Netherlands, in the north; the Rivierenland including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betuwe">Betuwe</a>, with orchards, in the southwest; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achterhoek">Achterhoek</a> in the east; and the city-region of Arnhem and Nijmegen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gelderland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Leicester left in 1587 the States General stopped seeking a monarch and governed as a republic of seven provinces, Holland, Zeeland, Utrecht, <b>Gelderland</b>, Overijssel, Friesland and Groningen, with conquered lands to the south ruled in common as the Generality Lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
